T02_settings_visuals_toggle.instruction:
Goal: add a boolean setting `visuals`, off by default (beta), that turns inline HTML visuals on and off.

Steps:
1. Follow the wallpaperLoop pattern end to end in src/shared/settings.ts:
   - the Settings field, with a doc comment;
   - a SettingsOp variant {type:'visuals', on:boolean};
   - the emptySettings default of false;
   - validation in applySettingsOp, returning the same object when nothing changes;
   - lenient parsing in parseSettings, counting dropped values.
2. Add a Row with a Switch in components/Settings.tsx, in the most fitting section (probably 'agent'). Use this about text: 'Agents may add small interactive HTML visuals (diagrams, comparisons, timelines) to replies. Renders sandboxed, offline. Applies to chats you open afterwards.'
3. Extend the SessionFeatures passed to SessionHost (src/main/index.ts builds it from settings) with `visuals`, so a spawned chat can read it. NONE gets visuals:false.
4. Add tests to src/shared/settings.test.ts (and src/main/settings.test.ts if relevant): the default, the op, invalid input, and parsing.

Out of scope: rendering and the prompt.
Acceptance: pnpm typecheck and pnpm test pass. Toggling the switch persists in settings.json and reaches the renderer store.

T04_visual_kit_runtime.instruction:
Goal: write the frame runtime and the small design system that make agent fragments look native to pi-gna. Without it they look like generic AI landing pages.

Runtime (one small vanilla JS file, no dependencies, no CDN):
- Receive {type:'render', html, tokens} from the parent and inject the fragment into #visual. Execute inline <script> tags in order: innerHTML does not run them, so recreate them.
- Apply the tokens as CSS variables on :root, and re-apply them on {type:'tokens'} when the theme changes.
- Report the content height with a ResizeObserver: {type:'height', px}.
- Intercept clicks on a[href] and send {type:'open-link', href} instead of navigating.
- Send uncaught errors and unhandledrejection as {type:'error', message}.
- Send a heartbeat if T01 chose a watchdog.

Kit CSS:
- Map pi-gna's tokens (--canvas --panel --raised --sunken --line --fg --muted --faint --accent --accent-soft --ok --bad --warn) and its sans and mono fonts.
- Use system fonts only. No shadows, gradients or rounded-pill excess. Match the transcript's density.
- Give a small vocabulary with restrained defaults:
  - .card, .grid (auto columns), .row, .stack;
  - .stat with .stat-value and .stat-label;
  - .table;
  - .badge with ok, bad, warn and accent variants;
  - .callout;
  - .steps, a numbered flow with arrows;
  - .timeline;
  - .legend;
  - .bar, a horizontal meter for comparisons;
  - .muted, .mono;
  - .controls for an optional input range or select;
  - an svg default that inherits currentColor and font.
- Width is the prose column, and nothing should exceed it horizontally.

Deliverables: the runtime and CSS files in the location set by the design. Also a fixture gallery (resources/visual/gallery.html or a docs page) that uses every component in light and dark.
Acceptance: the gallery renders correctly in both themes in the dev frame. The runtime has no external requests. Keep the CSS small; target under ~8 KB.

T05_markdown_visual_fence.instruction:
Goal: make src/renderer/src/lib/markdown.ts recognise the fence contract from the DESIGN.md Visuals section.

Steps:
1. Add an options argument to renderMarkdown and markdownToHtml, such as {visuals: boolean}. Keep every existing caller's behavior unchanged by default.
2. In the marked code renderer, when lang is `visual` and visuals is on and the body is within the 64 KB cap, emit a placeholder, for example `<div class="visual" data-visual="<index>"></div>`, and keep the fragment source retrievable by the component. Choose between an escaped source element hidden by CSS and a side array returned with the HTML; pick whichever survives DOMPurify cleanly. Whitelist the needed data- attributes via ADD_ATTR. The fragment itself must never go through innerHTML into the app document.
3. Otherwise, render it as a normal code block labelled `visual`: when visuals is off, when the cap is exceeded (add a short note), or when the fence is unterminated during streaming. Note: language `html` stays a plain code block; agents legitimately show HTML source.
4. Add unit tests in src/renderer/src/lib/lib.test.ts for:
   - on and off;
   - the cap;
   - multiple visuals in one message;
   - a visual inside a list or blockquote;
   - a ```visual fence nested in a 4-backtick fence, which must stay code;
   - script and iframe tags in the fragment, which must never appear in the sanitized app HTML.

Acceptance: pnpm test passes, and existing markdown tests are unchanged.
